WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.060
A newspaper's tip line
must disguise every voice,

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.340
so you move every tone
up three hundred hertz:

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.100
you multiply every sample
by a three hundred hertz tone.

4
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.500
Within the hour, the reporters call back:

5
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.300
there's a second voice under every caller,

6
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.680
just as loud, a little lower.

7
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.920
Where did it come from?

8
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.060
You only multiplied.

9
00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:18.750
Multiply one tone,

10
00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.430
a thousand hertz, for eight samples.

11
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.450
The product
is half a thirteen hundred hertz tone

12
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.830
plus half a seven hundred,
sample for sample.

13
00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.530
The tone split in two:
the upper half is the wanted copy,

14
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:28.830
the lower half the ghost.

15
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:30.280
Filter it away?

16
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.760
A voice runs from three hundred

17
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.000
to thirty-four hundred hertz,

18
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.220
so the ghosts land across the wanted copy:

19
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.740
the ghost of two thousand sits

20
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.960
at seventeen hundred,
on the wanted fourteen hundred.

21
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.840
No filter can tell them apart.

22
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.340
Back to lesson three's wheel:

23
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.420
a tone is a dot turning counter-clockwise,

24
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.660
and the line stores only
its left-right shadow.

25
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.380
Lesson six split a real tone
into two half-size arrows:

26
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.880
the top one counter-clockwise,
its mirror clockwise.

27
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.720
A clockwise dot is a negative tone,

28
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.380
and sounds like its positive twin.

29
00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.180
Multiplying arrows adds their turns:

30
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.360
thirteen and a half degrees
more per sample.

31
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.900
The top arrow speeds up,
to thirteen hundred hertz.

32
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.140
The mirror turns the other way,

33
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.080
so it slows down, to minus seven hundred,

34
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.640
which sounds at seven hundred.

35
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.920
The mirrors are the ghost.

36
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.220
So remove the mirrors first.

37
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.960
A call made only of top
arrows is the analytic signal.

38
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.840
For one tone, that's a full-size dot:
the cosine you store,

39
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.380
plus the sine, its up-down part.

40
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.420
The sine is the cosine
a quarter turn behind.

41
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.660
The filter that makes it
is the Hilbert transformer.

42
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.840
It turns positive tones back a quarter
turn and negative tones forward,

43
00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.580
so the line can still carry it.

44
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.160
Turn that output one more quarter
turn forward, and add the call.

45
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.680
The top arrow lands on itself: doubled.

46
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.680
The mirror lands opposite. Oh, gone.

47
00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.630
Now shift by one arrow,
three hundred hertz:

48
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.150
no mirrors to slow down.

49
00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.990
Keep the shadow:
the call times the cosine,

50
00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.950
minus the Hilbert output times the sine.

51
00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:48.770
A thousand hertz comes out
as thirteen hundred, digit for digit.

52
00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.010
One copy only: that's single sideband.

53
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.300
The Hilbert transformer's
coefficients never end,

54
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.200
like lesson ten's ideal filter.

55
00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.380
Keep thirty-one,
taper them with a Hann window:

56
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.200
it runs fifteen samples late.

57
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.400
Delay the plain call to match,
or the ghost is back:

58
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.360
forty-one percent at a thousand hertz.

59
00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.400
The target:
every ghost under five percent,

60
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.380
from three hundred
to thirty-four hundred hertz.

61
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.300
Thirty-one coefficients just miss:

62
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.380
five point two four
percent at three hundred.

63
00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.400
Thirty-five, with a delay of seventeen:
two point seven seven.

64
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:17.600
The ghost is gone.

65
00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:22.650
Next ticket: calls through a partner
network across the sea carry an echo.

66
00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.750
Every route has its own delay,
and you can't send the partner a click.
